  "show": "dix-pour-cent",
  "season": 1,
  "episode": 1,
  "scene": 31,
  "title": "Je vous garde  (I'm Keeping You)",
  "level": "A1 — débutant",
  "labels": {
    "andrea": "Andréa",
    "camille": "Camille"
  },
  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "But earlier, Andréa had also given her an almost impossible task: find out how much money Mathias was offered for one of his star actors. Camille found those figures anyway. Now she leaves them on Andréa's desk with a little note, and walks out. First, the whole scene in French. Relax and listen; nobody catches everything the first time.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Voilà. J'ai les chiffres que vous vouliez.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Au revoir, Andréa.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Je vous rappelle, d'accord?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Camille, attendez!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Richard, bonjour! Le Tchekhov était fabuleux.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Andréa? Mais je ne suis plus votre assistante.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Bon, donnez-moi les chiffres.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Tenez. Les voilà.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Et alors?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Je vous garde.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "C'est sûr?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Oui, c'est sûr.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Oh, merci! Merci, Andréa!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Je veux tellement ce poste!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Je vais tout donner!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Vous allez à votre bureau?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Oui! Et comment!",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let's take it slowly. Camille writes a short note, puts it on top of the figures, and quietly leaves.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Voilà.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'There.' Camille uses a tiny word for 'here it is' or 'that's it', heard in every French conversation.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "J'ai les chiffres que vous vouliez.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I have the figures you wanted.' Camille writes the formal 'you' to her boss.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Au revoir, Andréa.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Goodbye, Andréa.' The standard polite goodbye, literally 'until seeing again'. Camille heads for the stairs. Andréa, on the phone, sees the note.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Je vous rappelle, d'accord?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I'll call you back, okay?' She hangs up fast.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Camille, attendez!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Camille, wait!' This is a command with the formal 'you': it's simply the formal present form, used without the word for 'you'. Andréa runs down the stairs after her.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Richard, bonjour! Le Tchekhov était fabuleux.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Richard, hello! The Chekhov was fabulous.' Even while running, Andréa greets a man she knows from the theatre and praises a play by Chekhov. She never stops being an agent.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Andréa? Mais je ne suis plus votre assistante.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Andréa? But I'm not your assistant anymore.' Here, 'not anymore' is two little words around the action word: the first one, and then one that means 'more'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Bon, donnez-moi les chiffres.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Okay, give me the figures.' Another formal command.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Tenez. Les voilà.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Here. There they are.' The first word is what you say when you hand something to someone you call by the formal 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Et alors?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'So?' Literally 'and then?'. Camille waits while Andréa reads, with her heart beating.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Je vous garde.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I'm keeping you.' Just three words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "C'est sûr?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Really? For sure?' Literally 'it's sure?'. Camille can't believe it.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Oui, c'est sûr.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, for sure.' Andréa means it. The firing is cancelled.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Oh, merci! Merci, Andréa!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Oh, thank you! Thank you, Andréa!' In the show, Camille also lets a rude word slip out of pure joy. We kept the thank-yous.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Je veux tellement ce poste!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I want this job so much!' The verb is 'to want', and the word in the middle makes it 'so much'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Je vais tout donner!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I'm going to give it everything!' Here's a really useful pattern: the verb 'to go' plus another verb gives you the near future, just like 'going to' in English.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Vous allez à votre bureau?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you going to your desk?' The verb 'to go' again, this time on its own, with the formal 'you'. Andréa is telling her, without saying it, to get back to work.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Oui! Et comment!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes! You bet!' The last two words literally mean 'and how!', and they're a happy, very French way of saying 'absolutely'. Right, let's hear the whole scene once more, start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Voilà. J'ai les chiffres que vous vouliez.",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Au revoir, Andréa.",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Je vous rappelle, d'accord?",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Camille, attendez!",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Richard, bonjour! Le Tchekhov était fabuleux.",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Andréa? Mais je ne suis plus votre assistante.",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Bon, donnez-moi les chiffres.",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Tenez. Les voilà.",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Et alors?",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Je vous garde.",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "C'est sûr?",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Oui, c'est sûr.",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Oh, merci! Merci, Andréa!",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Je veux tellement ce poste!",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Je vais tout donner!",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "andrea",
      "t": "Vous allez à votre bureau?",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Oui! Et comment!",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "And that's how Camille got her job back. Don't worry if some lines went by quickly; you'll recognise the formal 'you' and the 'going to' future when you watch the episode. See you next time!",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "À demain, Andréa!",
      "gap": 0.3
    }
  ],
